# Button Card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The Button Card shows one entity as a tile. Tapping it toggles the entity, or opens its More info dialog when the entity cannot be toggled. In YAML it is `type: button`.

Use it for a single device or switch that people use often, such as a lamp or a door lock. For buttons you write yourself, see [Button](buttons.html).

This page covers:

- Button Card
  - `hui-button-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Inside one card:

1. An icon for the entity, which can be shown at a larger size.
2. The entity name.
3. The state, written as text, when you turn it on.

The whole card is the control. When it has a tap action, it behaves as a button and can be reached by keyboard.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-button-card"><code>hui-button-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: button
entity: light.reading_lamp
name: Reading lamp
show_state: true
tap_action:
  action: toggle
```

Keys:

- `entity` is the entity to show.
- `name` replaces the entity name, and `show_name` turns it on or off.
- `icon` replaces the icon, `show_icon` turns it on or off and `icon_height` sets its size, for example `80px`.
- `show_state` shows the state as text.
- `color` sets the colour of the icon when on. `none` turns the colour off. `state_color` is the older key.
- `tap_action`, `hold_action` and `double_tap_action` set what each gesture does.
- `theme` applies a theme to this card.

With no `tap_action`, the card toggles entities that can be toggled and opens More info for the rest.

Theme keys that reach it: `ha-card-border-radius` (square), `ha-card-border-width`, `ha-card-border-color`, `ha-card-box-shadow`, `ha-card-background`, `state-icon-color`, `state-active-color`, `state-inactive-color`, `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color`, `ha-font-size-l` and `ha-color-focus`.

By default Home Assistant shows keyboard focus on this card as a 1px coloured edge, and the icon grows slightly. Civica replaces that with a thicker dark outline and a gold ring, but only when the card-mod integration is installed. Without card-mod, the card keeps Home Assistant’s own focus look.

With card-mod you can set the corners and shadow for one card:

```yaml
type: button
entity: light.reading_lamp
card_mod:
  style: |
    ha-card { border-radius: 0; box-shadow: none; }
```

You cannot give this card primary or danger variants, add visible text beyond the name and state, or change the fact that it behaves as a button, with a theme or YAML.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One large target for a common action, which is quick to find on a phone.
- The name and state are real text, so the card can be read out.
- The colour of the icon shows the state, but the card does not check that this colour has enough contrast against the card. Measure it in your own theme.
- Hold and double-tap are gestures. The card does not promise that they work from a keyboard, so check them.
- The card needs at least two grid columns, so it cannot be squeezed smaller than that.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Show the name and the state in words. Do not rely on an icon or the icon colour alone, and do not turn the name off for an icon-only tile. Say “On” or “Locked”, not only a colour.

Make the tap action predictable. A tile that toggles a lamp should toggle it every time. Use More info for things that should not change with one touch, such as a lock or an alarm, and read [Dialogs](dialogs.html) for how confirmations stay in place.

Never make hold or double-tap the only way to do something. Put the same task on a visible control as well.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the card has the role of a button and its name is the entity name.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.5.1 Pointer Gestures and 2.5.2 Pointer Cancellation for hold and double-tap.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the state is also in words.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: measure the icon and the card edge.
- 2.5.3 Label in Name, and 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): measure the tile on your own dashboard.
- 1.4.4 and 1.4.10: check large text and a narrow screen.

Use a clear, short name. Keep the same action for the same kind of tile across the dashboard.

